Taints and Tolerations
•	Both Taints and Tolerations are used by the node to choose the pod (node perspective)
•	A way by which a node can refuse the pod
•	Taint is applied on the node and Toleration is applied on the pod
•	Syntax is kubectl taint node <name of node>key= Value: policy
•	Whatever policy applied in the node, same policy need to be applied in toleration in the pod .
•	2 different Policies
1.	No Schedule policy
•	If pod does not satisfy the condition, it will not schedule any pod, node will not accept any pod. Because none of the pods is showing toleration.
•	One toleration added to pod, node can be deployed that pod
•	If a pod already running in a node, after that any taint applied, In case the policy is No Schedule, then these pods won’t be touch.
•	New scheduling will be based on the taints

2.	No execute Policy
•	If a pod already running in a node, after that any taint applied, In case the policy is No Execute, then move existing pods to another node
•	No Execute policy is not recommended, due to this reason
•	Manager is tainted that is why no node is deployed in manager node
•	If 2 taints are there, both taint should be satisfied. Also Node selector also satisfied
# kubectl describe node manager | grep -I taint
 

